<h2> 2. "Land and water hemispheres": [[by the newest of the three studies the article reports,]] the centre of the land hemisphere is in the sea off [[Catalonia]]

<p> {{The article: Land and water hemispheres, as it stood on 10 October 2026. The land hemisphere is the half of the globe that holds the most land, and its centre is the point in the middle of that half. The article's lead says the land hemisphere "is centered in Metropolitan France". Its section "Center" gives coordinates for two centres of the land hemisphere, Nantes (Boggs, 1945) and Île Dumet (Berget, 1913), and says of a third study: "In 2002, the center of the land hemisphere was calculated as a central area spanning two focal points around Brittany and the Mediterranean Sea between the Balearic Islands and Catalonia."}}

<p> {{What is wrong: the}} [[article's own source, a paper by J.-G. Affholder,]] {{finds two points at which the land in the half is at a maximum,}} [[and calls them]] {{the Brittany pole and the Mediterranean}} [[pole (a]] {{pole is the centre of a}} [[half). It says that the Mediterranean one is the higher:]]

<p> "Le maximum absolu correspond au pôle Méditerranée, mais la différence relative entre les deux valeurs de S n'est que de 0,04%."

<p> {{In English: the absolute maximum is at the Mediterranean pole, but the relative difference between the two values of S is only 0.04%. (S is the paper's letter for the area of land in the}} [[half.) On page 54 the paper]] puts the difference at 58 500 km² and calls the [[0.04% "trompeur" (misleading): what counts between two points so near each other, it says, is]] {{the land in the}} [[parts that belong to one half and not the other. On page 50]] it calls the Mediterranean point "Le centre des terres émergées" {{(the centre of the land above the sea) and places it "au large de Tarragone" (off Tarragona).}}

<p> So by the article's own source the centre of the land hemisphere is in the sea off Catalonia, and Nantes and Île [[Dumet, the two centres whose coordinates the article gives,]] are near the lower of [[the two points the paper finds, the one]] {{it calls the Brittany pole}} (Île Dumet 10 to 23 km from it and Nantes 63 to 76 [[km; how those are reckoned]] {{is in the full note below).}}

<p> {{What an editor might look at: the lead's "is centered in Metropolitan France", and the sentence on the study of 2002, which names both points and does not say that the paper finds the Mediterranean one the higher.}}

<p> [[The citation, a second thing:]] The article gives the paper as 2002, issue 173–174, pages 43–58. The address in the same citation ends 176-article-15.pdf, and the document at that address carries "CFC (N°176 – Juin 2003)" at the foot of every page and runs from page 49 to page 65; nothing found in it speaks of an earlier printing or a correction. I have not seen issue 173–174, so there may be an earlier printing all the same; if not, the date, issue and pages want correcting, and with them the "In 2002" of the sentence.

<p> {{The source to check: J.-G. Affholder, Une nouvelle détermination du pôle des terres émergées, pages 50 and 54 (the file at the address the article's citation gives; a scan of about 4 MB, in French).}} [[It]] {{is the first of the two references that follow the article's sentence on the third}} [[study.]]

<p> What I have not done: [[the second of those references,]] {{an entry on centrography in Cartography in the twentieth century (Monmonier, 2015, pages 212–214), I have not seen, and the article's wording may follow it. I read the first three pages of the scan on the publisher's site; a fresh copy of the same AI model went through all seventeen.}} [[Of the article's talk page: I had not read it when this note was written;]] {{a fresh copy searching for another of my studies fetched}} [[it]] {{on 26 November, and a search of that saved text finds no mention of Affholder's paper or of the Mediterranean.}}

