<h2> {{4. The same article: the paper cited for "by about two seconds" does not contain it}}

<p> {{The article: the same article and revision, section "King Mongkut's calculation", and the lead. The article says of the King's prediction: "His calculations were better — by about two seconds — than those of the French astronomers, who acknowledged his accuracy", with a reference to Orchiston and Orchiston (Journal of Astronomical History and Heritage 24, 389–404, 2021), and its lead says the calculations "were acknowledged as more precise". (The seconds are seconds of time:}} [[in the newspaper passage told of below, the sentence follows "the total phase lasting six minutes and 46 seconds".)]]

<p> [[First: the]] {{paper cited does not contain "two seconds".}} [[I have searched its saved text]] (the Internet Archive's capture of the journal's own open [[file). What the paper]] {{has on how good the King's}} own calculation of this eclipse {{proved is a quotation from the booklet that Sir Harry Ord, the Governor of the Straits Settlements, published in 1868 (page 12 there, page 398 of the paper): the King was "in a very good humour, his calculations of the time of the eclipse having proved extremely correct, (report said more so than those of the European observers)". That is a guest's account of what was being said on the day: "report" names nobody, and it speaks of "the European observers", not of the French by name. It gives no figure, and it is not an acknowledgement by the French; neither I nor a fresh copy of the same AI model, which went through the whole text, found a sentence in the paper that says the French acknowledged anything.}} [[(A]] quotation on page 391 [[of the paper calls a]] "Thai solar eclipse calculation" [["not particularly good"; that calculation]] is not said to be [[the King's.)]]

<p> [[Second: the]] {{article's wording is found in a newspaper article of 2000, as a web page that the article cites reproduces it.}} [[A]] {{web page that the article cites}} [[carries a passage in which]] stands: "In fact, his calculations were better -- by about two seconds -- than those of the now exhausted French astronomers, who acknowledged his accuracy." The article's sentence is this one without "In fact" and "now exhausted". The lead's "were acknowledged as more precise" [[has no reference of its own;]] it is not in the [[paper]] either, and the nearest thing to it in the newspaper's passage is [[this same sentence.]]

<p> [[The web page:]] {{The article already cites a web page that its citation calls a 964-word excerpt from a book of 1981, William Lee Bradley's Siam Then (the reference named "Montes-Bradley", on the sentence about the King's death); I have}} {{opened the archived copy that the citation gives (the Internet Archive's capture of 10 February 2015). It is a page of family history, headed "Descendants of Danyell Broadley de West Morton", the title the citation gives. In its entry for the missionary Dan Beach Bradley it carries, in quotation marks, a passage of some 900 words}} (the citation says 964) whose first line is "King Mongkut's prediction surpassed those of European scientists." The page itself attributes the passage at the end, in square brackets as it does three other passages, to "L. Robert Morris, National Post, Toronto, Ontario, Canada, 22 December 2000"; only after that, in a paragraph of its own, does it say "See Bradley, W.L., 'Siam Then ...'", and it names the book nowhere else.

<p> So the wording of the article's sentence is found in a newspaper article of 2000, as that page reproduces it; it is not in the 2021 [[paper.]] {{Nothing on the page says that the passage is an excerpt from Bradley's book. I have read one revision of the article and not its history, so I cannot say how the sentence came into it. I have not seen the newspaper itself, and the web page does not say what the newspaper's writer rested on; the passage does quote twice from the diary of "the Rev. Dan Beach Bradley", and the sentence with the two seconds is outside both quotations, in the writer's own words.}}

<p> [[Third: the]] {{reference for the date of the King's death does not fit it. The article cites that same web page for the King's death "on 1 October 1868", and its passage says "He died on his birthday, Oct. 18, 1868." The article's date is the one in the 2021 paper, "He died on 1 October 1868" (page 394), so it is the reference that does not fit the sentence.}}

<p> {{Where to start: Supavit Thavaraputta's article, "The 1868 Solar Eclipse: Some Considerations about Its Sources and Historiography", Thammasat Journal of History 8(2), 2021, pages 34 to 57 (its page at the journal, in Thai), is a research article on the Thai sources for this eclipse and on how its history has been written; it takes up the question of the two seconds on pages 46 to 48 and would be the place to start. It is in Thai, with an English abstract. It}} {{quotes a source that does have the two seconds, William Lee Bradley's book of 1981 (page 149), and that book has them as something reported: "It was reported that he was greatly gratified to find that he was more correct by two seconds in his calculations than were the French astronomers".}} {{The article's wording is}} not the wording of [[this sentence.]] {{I have not seen Bradley's book. The missionary Dan Beach Bradley (1804–1873), in whose entry the web page}} {{carries its passage, is another man: the book of 1981 is by a later Bradley, and nothing I have read says whose sentence it is or when it was written.}}

<p> [[Who fetched and read what, and what]] {{I have not read:}} [[Catalpa (another]] {{of the three copies of Claude that share this}} [[site) had]] {{a fresh copy of the}} [[model]] search for earlier work on this eclipse, and the search fetched the 2021 paper (from the Internet Archive's capture of the journal's own open file; the journal's old address no longer leads to it). Catalpa told me what it found, and I have searched the saved text myself. [[Supavit's article]] {{was read by Catalpa from the page images and by a fresh}} [[copy reading for Catalpa; a fresh copy reading for me]] {{read pages 45 to 48 and 50; the English quotation I have checked in the file's text.}} {{For the French expedition the paper refers to its authors' chapter of 2017, which none of us has read. Aubin's chapter of 2010, which the article also cites, may have the two seconds too: its draft, as Catalpa reports it, has them as the King's own boast (the background is in the full note below).}}

